{"name":"Red River","hex":"#b95543","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Red River","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Various rivers around the world"]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A river in northern Vietnam and Yunnan, southern China."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Red_River"}],"entries":[{"title":"Red River of the South","blocks":[{"text":"The Red River is a major river in the Southern United States. It was named for its reddish water color from passing through red-bed country in its watershed. It is known as the Red River of the South to distinguish it from the Red River of the North, which flows between Minnesota and North Dakota into the Canadian province of Manitoba. Although once a tributary of the Mississippi River, the Red River now is a tributary of the Atchafalaya River, a distributary of the Mississippi that flows separately into the Gulf of Mexico. This confluence is connected to the Mississippi River by the Old River Control Structure."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Redriverbonhamtx.jpg/330px-Redriverbonhamtx.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_River_of_the_South","wikidata":"Q156032"},{"title":"Red River of the North","blocks":[{"text":"The Red River, also called the Red River of the North to differentiate it from the Red River in the south of the continent, is a river in the north-central United States and the Canadian Prairies. Originating at the confluence of the Bois de Sioux and Otter Tail rivers between the U.S. states of Minnesota and North Dakota, it flows northward through the Red River Valley, forming most of the border of Minnesota and North Dakota and continuing into Manitoba. It empties into Lake Winnipeg, whose waters join the Nelson River and ultimately flow into Hudson Bay."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Esplanade_Riel_Bridge_at_dusk%2C_Winnipeg%2C_Canada.jpg/330px-Esplanade_Riel_Bridge_at_dusk%2C_Winnipeg%2C_Canada.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_River_of_the_North","wikidata":"Q156006"},{"title":"Red River (manga)","blocks":[{"text":"Red River , also known as Anatolia Story, is a Japanese manga series written and illustrated by Chie Shinohara. It was published by Shogakukan in their Sho-Comi magazine from January 1995 to June 2002. It is published in English in North America by Viz Media."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/b8/Red_River%2C_Volume_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_River_(manga)","wikidata":"Q488129"},{"title":"River","blocks":[{"text":"A river is a natural stream of fresh water that flows on land or inside caves towards another body of water at a lower elevation, such as an ocean, lake, or another river. A river may run dry before reaching the end of its course if it runs out of water, or only flow during certain seasons. Rivers are regulated by the water cycle, the processes by which water moves around the Earth. Water first enters rivers through precipitation, whether from rainfall, the runoff of water down a slope, the melting of glaciers or snow, or seepage from aquifers beneath the surface of the Earth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg/330px-Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River","wikidata":"Q4022"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/RedRiverMeandersArkansas1.jpg/500px-RedRiverMeandersArkansas1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/RedRiverMeandersArkansas1.jpg/1280px-RedRiverMeandersArkansas1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/RedRiverMeandersArkansas1.jpg/500px-RedRiverMeandersArkansas1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Point bars, abandoned meander loops, oxbow lakes in Lafayette and Miller counties, Arkansas","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARedRiverMeandersArkansas1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/State_Highway_78_Bridge_at_the_Red_River.jpg/500px-State_Highway_78_Bridge_at_the_Red_River.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/State_Highway_78_Bridge_at_the_Red_River.jpg/1280px-State_Highway_78_Bridge_at_the_Red_River.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/State_Highway_78_Bridge_at_the_Red_River.jpg/500px-State_Highway_78_Bridge_at_the_Red_River.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"State Highway No. 78 Bridge at the Red River between Oklahoma and Texas, photographed on the Oklahoma side","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AState_Highway_78_Bridge_at_the_Red_River.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/HPIM2184.JPG/500px-HPIM2184.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/HPIM2184.JPG/1280px-HPIM2184.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/HPIM2184.JPG/500px-HPIM2184.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbn